export const KNOWLEDGE_MENTORING_PATTERNS = "Knowledge Base\n\nHow Mizuho Gives Feedback on New Ventures\n\n\nSource\n\nThese are Mizuho's own mentoring notes, written while mentoring around 40 new venture teams and while judging pitch events, plus written messages she sent to founders. The notes were originally in Japanese and covered specific ventures in education, mobility, counselling, fishing, parking and child safety. The domain details have been removed on purpose. What is kept is the reasoning that repeats across all of them.\n\n\n1. Understanding the user\n\nDemographics and circumstances are not understanding. Knowing someone's age and situation says nothing about how they decide.\n\n- What does this person actually value? What drives their decisions?\n- What do they want to gain? What state are they trying to end up in?\n- Within the same target group, are there different needs?\n- Who is the first customer? Are you aiming at several targets at once?\n- Is this user built out of your own imagination, or checked against real people?\n- Are you thinking about what you would do, or about what that person would do?\n\nQuestions she actually asks:\n\"What does this person really want to get?\"\n\"Would that actually make them happy?\"\n\"What is the single hardest thing for this person?\"\n\"Who is the first customer?\"\n\n\n2. Finding the real pain\n\nThis is the one that comes up most often in the notes.\n\nDo not treat the visible difficulty as the real problem. Go one layer down.\n\nIf the stated problem is that information is missing, giving information may not fix it. Is the problem that the information does not exist, that it exists but they cannot judge, that they have nobody to ask, or that something psychological or structural is in the way?\n\n- When several problems are listed, are they really separate? Or the same root cause in three different words?\n- What was actually hard about it?\n- What is the real bottleneck?\n- What would the user spend time or money to solve?\n- If they have already tried something and it did not work, where exactly did it break down?\n- Does this action need to happen at all? Can the premise itself be questioned?\n- Does the inconvenience the user feels match the problem that actually needs solving?\n\nQuestions she actually asks:\n\"What was actually hard about it?\"\n\"What is the bottleneck here?\"\n\"Is that really it?\"\n\"Would having the information actually solve it?\"\n\"Why does this have to happen at all?\"\n\"What would they pay to fix?\"\n\n\n3. Narrowing\n\nTrying to solve everything means nothing gets solved sharply.\n\n- Covering more problems is not automatically more value to the user.\n- Pick one problem. Make the solution sharp and deep.\n- Compare how important the problems are. Something being tedious does not make it the top problem.\n- Does the user's priority match the builder's priority?\n- Does solving this create a new problem? Does adding another option just add to the user's burden?\n\nQuestions she actually asks:\n\"Which one is the biggest problem?\"\n\"Is that really the thing they struggle with most?\"\n\"Do you have to solve all of it?\"\n\"If you could only solve one, which one?\"\n\n\n4. Is the solution the right one\n\nThink about what value the user needs before thinking about features.\n\n- Is this really the best way to solve that problem?\n- Are you confusing having a feature with being valuable?\n- Could something else solve the same problem?\n- Why are existing services or workarounds not enough?\n- Does a solution need to be provided at all? Could the behaviour change instead?\n- What is the single most important value in this solution? Can everything else be cut for now?\n- Is the user's benefit mixed up with what is convenient for the provider?\n\nQuestions she actually asks:\n\"Is that solution really the best one?\"\n\"Why does it have to be this?\"\n\"Why not some other way?\"\n\"What is the core value, in the end?\"\n\n\n5. Hypotheses, research and validation\n\nHer position here is explicit and she has written it out at length.\n\nA hypothesis is not made to be right. It is made to make clear what needs to be tested.\n\n- Personas, user journeys and points of view are not deliverables that express the correct answer.\n- They exist to make visible what you know and what you do not.\n- Separate guesses from facts backed by primary research.\n- Write down what you do not know, what you are saying on instinct, and which assumptions are unverified.\n- Align hypotheses across the team. If members hold different premises, the solution never settles.\n- Writing the hypothesis down is what makes interview questions specific.\n- Do not validate something vague like \"the problem\". Validate specific behaviour, specific feelings, specific conditions.\n- Do not treat the number of interviews as the goal. Asking the same hypothesis repeatedly stops producing learning.\n- Update the hypothesis after each round, then test a new one.\n- Spending tens of hours debating a hypothesis is worse than testing quickly, but increasing the number of tests without thinking is also wrong.\n\nThe reasoning she gives when founders ask why hypotheses matter at all:\n\nWe are not the user, so discussing among ourselves has a limit. Noticing that is healthy. But guessing is not useless, because the purpose of guessing is not to be right, it is to know what to go and ask.\n\nWithout a hypothesis, interviews drift, questions are improvised, and findings get interpreted in whatever way is convenient. She has seen teams say they interviewed ten people and then be unable to name a single hypothesis that got tested.\n\nShe uses the usability finding that testing one hypothesis with about five people surfaces most of the main problems. The point is not that five interviews are enough. The point is that with the same premise and the same hypothesis, learning drops off sharply after five people. That is why updating the hypothesis matters. The cycle is: go from zero to about eighty percent understanding, update the hypothesis, then go from zero to eighty again on the new one. That repeated cycle is what iteration means. The often quoted figure of two hundred validations does not mean two hundred interviews in a row. It means that cycle, two hundred times, with the hypothesis revised each time.\n\nFrom her own experience: ten interviews with a defined hypothesis taught her more than fifty interviews without one.\n\nQuestions she actually asks:\n\"Is that a fact or a guess?\"\n\"What is it that you do not know right now?\"\n\"What are you going to go and ask?\"\n\"What would have to be true for this hypothesis to hold?\"\n\"Which assumption would hurt most if it turned out to be wrong?\"\n\"What do you want to learn from this interview?\"\n\n\n6. MVP and field tests\n\nAn MVP is not a polished product. It is a way to answer the hypothesis you want to test.\n\n- Decide what you want to validate first.\n- Then work out the minimum needed to validate that.\n- Analogue, fake, manually operated, all fine.\n- Do not stuff several hypotheses into one MVP.\n- Testing one at a time is what lets you tell which part was right and which part was wrong.\n- Design it so the result tells you how far back you need to go.\n- A user saying \"nice\" or \"I would want that\" is not evidence of need. They carry no risk when they say it.\n- Look for an actual booking, sign up, purchase or use, something where the user commits cost or effort.\n\nQuestions she actually asks:\n\"What do you want to validate?\"\n\"What is the minimum you need to check that?\"\n\"Could you test this with a fake version?\"\n\"Why mix them all into one test?\"\n\"Did the user actually do something?\"\n\n\n7. Getting to users, and insisting on primary sources\n\nNothing beats hearing it from the user. Not being able to reach users is not an excuse.\n\n- Finding interviewees is part of the job of building a venture.\n- Use your network, social media, events, communities, existing relationships.\n- Contact experts directly. Do not be too afraid of being refused.\n- If you have no budget, work out how to reach them without money.\n- Go to where the target group already is, and keep showing up.\n- Building those relationships also builds your first customers later.\n- Surveys and secondary sources have their place. Do not use them as a substitute for primary research.\n- What matters is not that interviews happened, but what was learned.\n\nFrom her own practice: she joined two startup programmes and interviewed the other founders, used her own network and social media, attended events on the topic to build relationships with potential users, asked for announcement slots when speaking at events, and emailed academics and well known mentors directly. Some of it was unpaid and she was told off occasionally, but her view was that being embarrassed or disliked costs nothing compared to the problem she wanted to solve. Eighty to ninety percent of it was legwork rather than paid platforms, because there was no budget.\n\nHer mentor's instruction, which she repeats: you are a founder now, so do not look for reasons you cannot do it. Work out how to meet users without spending money, and go where they are, regularly, and build the relationship yourself.\n\nQuestions she actually asks:\n\"How could you actually meet that user?\"\n\"Are you sure you cannot reach them?\"\n\"Is there a way to do it without money?\"\n\"Have you gone to where those people actually are?\"\n\n\n8. Market, viability and competition\n\nBeing socially meaningful and being a business are two different things.\n\n- A socially correct problem is not necessarily one people pay to solve.\n- Doing it as a volunteer and doing it as a business are different. Be clear which one this is.\n- Does the user pay, or does someone else pay?\n- Separate the customer, the user, the decision maker and the payer.\n- When sizing the market, check whether limiting it to the current target or location is hiding a much larger one.\n- A large market does not mean you can enter it. Look at competitors and existing workarounds.\n- The absence of an existing service is not evidence of need.\n- If services do exist, find out why the problem is still unsolved and what is insufficient about them.\n- Question whether the axes you are comparing competitors on are the right axes.\n- Consider whether future technology or social change will dissolve the problem itself.\n- Is this a business that depends on a temporary gap, or does it hold its own value long term?\n- A venture built to take investment and a small profitable business need different strategies.\n\nQuestions she actually asks:\n\"Who pays?\"\n\"Would they really pay to have this solved?\"\n\"Why has nobody solved this already?\"\n\"Are those the right axes to compare against?\"\n\"Will this service still be needed in the future?\"\n\n\n9. Ethics, impact and responsibility\n\nThe notes show she weighs honesty toward the user as heavily as viability.\n\nWill the social problem you named actually be solved? Does the solution create new harm?\n\n- You may deliver value to the user in front of you, but could someone else be disadvantaged?\n- Does the structure make things worse for people who cannot afford it?\n- If someone uses the product and the problem still happens, does that leave them with false expectations or misplaced blame?\n- The psychology and behaviour the provider assumes may not match reality.\n- Do not design on the assumption of common sense or good intentions. Understand how the people involved actually behave.\n- The more sensitive the subject, the more the wording, marketing, delivery and social side effects have to be thought through.\n- Hold the social vision and the need to make money at the same time.\n- Decide direction by thinking hard about what is good for the user, and let the business follow from that.\n\nA concrete case she gives: with a safety device, the presence of a camera is said to deter crime. But while only a small number are distributed, the harm moves to a different target or place rather than disappearing. And if only people who can afford it are protected, the people who cannot are targeted more. Raising the price is better for the business and worse ethically. She never fully resolved this. On top of that, some offenders said a camera made it more exciting rather than deterring them, and some said they rehearsed turning the situation around so the victim looks like the problem. People without that distortion cannot imagine that reasoning. So if the vision is to remove the harm, you have to think the whole thing through, rather than assume the obvious mechanism works.\n\nQuestions she actually asks:\n\"Is anyone disadvantaged by this?\"\n\"Does this actually solve the problem itself?\"\n\"What happens to the people who cannot have it?\"\n\"Are you giving the user a false expectation?\"\n\"Is this genuinely good for the user?\"\n\n\n10. Why this person, and why here\n\nTo keep a venture going, the people doing it have to be convinced themselves about why they are doing this, and why in this place.\n\n- Why do you want to do this? What do you actually want?\n- Why do you want to solve that problem?\n- Why does it need to be this organisation, this company, this environment?\n- Did you choose this environment because it is the best option for the user?\n- When the company's interest and the user's interest collide, how will you decide?\n- Do you understand the cost you are taking on, in time, money and mental health?\n- Is the whole team facing the same direction?\n- Are your backers and investors people who share the values, not just the funding?\n\nThe user side of it: the user does not care whose company it is. In house, startup, side project, student project, none of it matters to them. What builds trust is whether you chose the option you believed was best for the user.\n\nA related position: when someone asked her for her research materials so they could skip the validation work, she said the materials could be handed over, but that most of the content was primary research she had gathered on her own feet, which is exactly why it carried weight in a pitch. Quoted second hand it loses that. She asked them to doubt the material and verify it themselves, because what they needed at that point was not information but clarity about what they were actually trying to achieve.\n\nQuestions she actually asks:\n\"Why do you want to do this?\"\n\"If you really thought about the user, is this still the best place to do it?\"\n\"Can you say this was your own choice?\"\n\n\n11. Feedback on pitches\n\nShe looks at delivery as well as content, and at what makes the venture attractive, not only at what is weak.\n\n- Does the logic hold? Are the claims connected to the evidence?\n- Is there too much information? What can be cut?\n- Does the audience understand how much weight each number or claim carries?\n- Is there a real texture of user voices and lived experience, or only generalities and secondary sources?\n- Does the seriousness of the problem and the size of the market land concretely?\n- Is it clear where the interest and appeal of this venture actually is?\n- Improve the weak parts, but also find what is good and push it further.\n- Does the presenter's own experience and personality come through as part of the appeal?\n- Is it clear at the end what you want to say and what you are asking for?\n\nHer own pitch feedback criteria, written for a pitch event:\n1. Is the problem clearly defined?\n2. Is the need to solve it clearly explained?\n3. Is the proposed solution appropriate and effective?\n4. Did the concept and solution come across clearly?\n5. Did the delivery hold the audience?\n\n\nCross cutting patterns\n\nQuestion the premise. Is this really a problem, and does this really need to happen?\nGo one layer down. Look past the surface inconvenience to the psychology, behaviour and structure behind it.\nNarrow sharply. Do not solve everything. Find the one that matters.\nStart from the user. Value is defined from their side, not from what is convenient to build.\nInsist on primary sources. Confirm actual behaviour and actual words, not imagination or generalities.\nSeparate hypothesis from fact. Make assumptions visible so they can be tested.\nTest small. Validate with the minimum before building the finished thing.\nLook at viability. Social meaning is not enough. Revenue, market and sustainability matter.\nThink about side effects. Check whether the solution creates new harm or new exclusion.\nTake the person's own conviction seriously. Whether they believe in it themselves is part of whether this survives.\nGrow the strengths. Point out what is genuinely interesting, not only what is weak.";

/** From knowledge/Knowledge_LectureMaterial.md (42512 chars) */
export const KNOWLEDGE_LECTURE_MATERIAL = "Knowledge Base, part 2\n\nTeaching material Mizuho wrote for new venture workshops\n\n\nSource and how to use it\n\nLecture decks Mizuho wrote and delivered for new venture workshops and for client teams, plus the pitch Q&A template she built for teams to rehearse against. Japanese phrasing is kept where it carries her voice.\n\nThree rules for using this material.\n\nThe standard frameworks included here are the theories she cites as grounding when she mentors. Use them as support for a point about this person's idea. Never recite a framework on its own as the answer.\n\nShe does also teach that design thinking carries through into business implementation. That is background, not a feedback line. Do not answer anyone by telling them design thinking is useful for business.\n\nThe named Japanese startups in the pitch material are not well known outside Japan. Refer to them as a Japanese startup rather than by name.\n\n\nPART A. Defining the problem\n\n\nBusiness value is decided by the problem, not the solution\n\nBusiness value is almost entirely decided by the problem.\n\nBefore thinking about how to solve a problem correctly, think about whether you are solving the correct problem at all. Whether the problem exists, and whether it has been framed properly, is what matters.\n\n\nWhy solving many problems at once fails\n\nTeams ask her: to grow the business, shouldn't we solve lots of problems for lots of people at once? Her answer is no, for two reasons.\n\nIt wastes time and money, and the waste compounds. Different problems need different solutions. Building something that covers all of them takes time and money. The more you have spent, the harder it becomes to stop, so the damage grows. And while you are spending that time, competitors get there first.\n\nIt makes differentiation impossible. A solution that only half-fits a bunch of different problems doesn't get chosen. Her strongest line on this: A product or service built to answer every single customer problem ends up liked by no one, even though it's also hated by no one. There is no almighty solution. Trying to please everyone produces something nobody is pleased by.\n\nHer method: Start by narrowing the focus of the problem. Expanding after that to reach more people is the standard path. Narrow first, expand later.\n\n\nProblem and solution as overlapping circles\n\nShe draws this as a Venn diagram. The more the problem and the solution overlap, the more value there is. If the problem does not exist, there is no value no matter how good the solution. If they sit apart, there is no value either.\n\nThe same picture explains over-specification. A solution built beyond what the problem needs does not extend the overlap, so it does not add value.\n\nAnd it explains why a mixed-together solution fails. If you combine the car, the refrigerator and the telephone into one thing, you get something that makes no sense and overlaps nothing properly.\n\n\nBreaking a big problem down\n\nAs long as it stays a \"big problem,\" it's hard to solve right now at a reasonable cost.\n\nBreak the large problem into solvable smaller ones, then identify which of those has the largest effect.\n\nThe nuance she adds, which is the important part: This doesn't mean gambling on whether narrowing down to one guess hits the mark. It's more like putting down your strongest candidate and using it as material to find out from the user how different it actually is from the problem they really have, and by how much. Whichever one you pick, it's almost always off by some amount. The goal isn't to \"hit\" the right answer, it's to use the hypothesis, assuming there will be a gap, as material to clarify what's on target and what's different, and by how much.\n\nNarrowing is not a bet on being right. It is a probe. You will be off by some amount. The point is to find out by how much and in which direction. And if the point you picked turns out to be wrong, having mapped the whole picture lets you aim at another part of it.\n\n\nHow to draw the problem out, as a tree\n\nPut the large problem on the left, and branch it out to the right, breaking it down, the way a logic tree is built.\n\nAsk Why so to grow the branches to the right and go deeper. If you only have specifics and cannot see the larger problem above them, ask So what to derive it.\n\n\nBe a good doctor, symptom versus cause\n\nThis is how she frames it. Someone arrives saying their stomach hurts. Is a doctor who immediately hands over stomach medicine a good doctor? No. A good doctor works out the cause behind the symptom. If it is cancer, stomach medicine will not work and chemotherapy is needed. If it is appendicitis, surgery is needed. If it is period pain, a painkiller is needed. What matters is not the symptom but the cause.\n\nWhat matters is identifying not the \"symptom\" but the \"cause of the illness.\"\n\nA bad doctor prescribes for the symptom, which in business means looking at the surface phenomenon and reaching for an obvious fix. Find the cause and treat that instead.\n\nBusiness works exactly the same way. Take the problem that there is not enough information for changing jobs. That is a symptom. The easy move is to launch a job information site, but remember the doctor.\n\nGo one step past the symptom and think about the cause. If the cause is that the person cannot define what information they need, so they feel information is lacking, then the solution might be a coaching service that makes clear what they need and what conditions they want. If instead the cause is that job postings are written too generically to judge fit, then the solution might be a service that fills in company culture and ways of working through video and interviews.\n\n\nMacro market problem versus micro customer problem\n\nMake sure not to confuse the vague macro problem of \"the market\" with the micro problem of the one customer actually in front of you.\n\nIt's not \"the market\" that pays money, it's the customer. If you don't understand the customer's problem, you can't offer the right product, and you can't solve the market's problem, the symptom, either.\n\n\nMost new ventures are not deep enough\n\nMost new ventures, basically, just aren't deep enough.\n\nHer checklist of what a shallow problem statement looks like:\n\nGeneric and abstract. fuzzy, vague So abstract that it applies to anything and all you can say back is \"sure, I suppose\". For example, saying the IT industry is struggling. Of course it is struggling. Struggling with what.\n\nA problem you hear all the time. Problems that come up in every other industry too, or that apply to everyone. Not enough information. Matching does not work well.\n\nNo specifics. Cannot give the 5W1H, who is hitting this problem when and where, and how they currently cope with it. Cannot give a concrete instance. Cannot say \"for example in the case of this particular person\".\n\nNeeds a long explanation, and the solution is naive. If it takes a long explanation for the problem or the solution to land, the structure has not been sorted out and the important point has not been identified. Not enough information so let's build a media site. Not enough people so let's build a matching service.\n\nWeak reading of the competition. Claiming to beat everyone on every axis. Normally this kind of thing doesn't happen, so there's usually something overlooked in the depth, breadth, or structure of the problem. Or claiming there are no competitors. If the problem really exists, someone would already be solving it by some other means. If there really are no competitors, it might be because the problem isn't actually worth solving.\n\n\nHer own scale for depth\n\nNot an absolute definition, her own yardstick, offered so teams can try measuring themselves against it.\n\nLevels 1 to 3, gathering information through internet and literature research.\nLevels 3 to 5, digging in through user interviews.\nLevels 4 to 6, observation and insight focused on individuals.\n\nAim for level 6 or 7 and above.\n\nAfter surveying broadly, pick the subset of customers who have acute pain or feel urgency.\n\n\nPART B. Target user\n\n\nWhy the persona has to be defined\n\nTo decide the recruiting criteria for interviews. To raise the resolution of the user so the prototype can be built accurately. So that interviews can test whether the target user hypothesis is right. And so that sales, marketing and branding can move quickly and accurately when it becomes a real business.\n\nHer framing is resolution. If you move forward with the target user still fuzzy, you'll fall into a trap. Low resolution means no leads, no pipeline, no deals, no revenue.\n\n\nWhy you cannot serve everyone, with her counter-example\n\nTeams push back: we want to reach everybody, why narrow down? Her answer is that even a category like \"engineers in companies\" is not one persona. A programmer at a hardware maker just wants to finish the work fast. An engineering manager at a mobile game company likes new things. A CTO at a startup has to respond flexibly to change and has little money. A mid-career engineer at a foreign IT company grew up abroad and struggles with Japanese working style and conversation. Different users mean different problems.\n\n\nDiffusion of innovation, as she uses it\n\nThe standard curve, which she cites as grounding for why order of adoption matters.\n\nInnovators, 2.5 percent. They want to try the new thing first.\nEarly adopters, 13.5 percent. They look the new thing over carefully and then buy it willingly.\nEarly majority, 34 percent. Rather than judging for themselves, they actively buy what is trending and what other people are using.\nLate majority, 34 percent. Reluctant to buy as a rule, and only start using it once everyone else is.\nLaggards, 16 percent. They do not want to take any risk at all, and only move once the thing has become an ordinary, established part of life.\n\nHer point is that products spread in this order, phases need different tactics, and you cannot skip a stage.\n\n\nWho to target first\n\nHer question: Who is the customer who will use this first and cry with joy over it?\n\nHer markers for an early adopter, which are markers of someone ready to buy rather than a theoretical category:\n\nThey have a serious problem that is not being solved. Not merely that a problem exists, but that it is biting right now and cannot be left alone.\nThey are aware of it as their own problem. They can put it into words and explain it to someone, although some of the insight may still be unconscious.\nThey have already started actively looking for a solution. Not \"it would be nice if\", but actively searching, comparing, considering.\nThey have cobbled together a workaround out of several tools or manual processes. They are already paying a cost in money or effort.\nThey are willing to pay and are actually able to buy. Not just wanting it, but able to take the action, or already taking it.\n\n\nPersona is not segmentation\n\nSegmentation cuts by attributes, age, job, industry, and focuses on what is different between groups. Attributes alone do not determine preference or behaviour.\n\nA persona focuses on the sameness of values and goals that exists across segments. Focus on the sameness in values and goals that exists across segments. Choosing a persona whose needs and goals can spread across segments is how you end up with enough market, rather than by widening the attribute net.\n\n\nStereotype is not archetype\n\nA stereotype is a flat simplification of someone you can picture, too abstract and not grounded.\n\nAn archetype is a personality built on distinctive repeated experience and values, with its own specific context for why they want what they want. A customer image that could apply to anyone is meaningless.\n\n\nProvisional personas before research\n\nIt is legitimate to make a provisional persona before you have the research, but with conditions. Do not build it from assumption or from the customer you would find convenient. Use whatever real accumulated understanding of customers you already have. Treat it as provisional, build a prototype from it, test the prototype, and feed what you learn back into the persona. The loop is: build the persona from deep customer understanding, prototype from it, test in interviews, update to the correct understanding.\n\n\nWhat a strong persona contains\n\nA photograph, chosen last. To avoid building a stereotyped persona that gets pulled along by appearance, it's important to decide this last, after everything else is created. Avoid famous faces.\nDemographic information, noting that the grouping itself carries little meaning since attributes do not determine preference or behaviour. It is there to make the picture concrete.\nOne line that captures this persona, a keyword or short statement that makes them memorable and easy to feel.\nThe goal, which is the most important element. What the user cares about is not using the tool but reaching the goal. Write the reason behind the goal, not only what and how but why. Note: the user's goal isn't always simply \"being able to do something easily, conveniently, and efficiently.\"\nA living story, the persona in context including the time when they are not using the service.\n\nTwo rules when writing one up: change the name from the real interviewee, and do not jump straight to writing the solution.\n\n\nPART C. Solution\n\n\nOver-specification adds nothing\n\nHer test question: if you take the same problem and build a product with cutting edge technology and superb specifications, how much does the value increase? Her answer: it does not. Even if you build an over-specified solution for the problem, the value it generates is the same as what a \"sufficient solution\" would generate.\n\n\nThe do-everything solution trap\n\nA solution that does many things usually grazes the problem the user wants solved, fits it only slightly, and loses to the alternatives on comparison. It may genuinely beat other solutions on some axis, but an axis the customer does not care about, while losing on the axis they do care about. For a do-everything solution to be valued, it has to win on an axis the user cares about first, and then also be good enough on the others, which is hard.\n\n\nThree conditions for a good solution\n\nIt solves the problem sufficiently.\nIt is achievable now, at a reasonable cost.\nIt is better than the alternatives.\n\nWhen you cannot decide what to show in a prototype, go back to these three.\n\n\nThe tree that organises value and function\n\nThree levels, read from the top down, getting more concrete as you go.\n\nAt the top, the core value the service should deliver.\n\nBelow it, the PoV. Why our service can deliver the core value is because it can do [1] and [2]. Two reasons why this service can deliver that value.\n\nBelow that, the functions. The functions needed to make that happen are [   ]. What actually has to be built for those reasons to hold.\n\nThe claim at the top is supported from underneath by reasons, and the reasons by functions. Starting from the problem, let's organize the evidence that shows it ends up this way.\n\nHer checklist, and the instruction that goes with it. Does the PoV match the core value. Can the solution really deliver the core value. Does the meaning connect all the way from top to bottom without a jump. (Carefully, one step at a time, like doing a math proof)\n\n\nSaying the business in one sentence\n\nIn the situation of [situation], this is [product name], a [product category] for [target customer] who has the problem of [problem]. It has the benefit of [benefit]. Unlike existing alternatives and competitors, it comes with [differentiator].\n\nIf you cannot compress it, the logic is not clear yet.\n\n\nPART D. Assumptions, interviews and testing\n\n\nAssumptions are the premises underneath the problem\n\nA hypothesis is what forms the underlying premise of the \"problem\" you're assuming.\n\nTeams do not notice that what they think is the problem rests on several unexamined assumptions about the user and their situation. When those break, you get the result . Yes it is a problem in the abstract, but it almost never comes up in reality.\n\n\nThe transport illustration, which is an illustration and not a case\n\nThis is a made up example she uses to show that when the problem changes, the solution changes. It is not something that happened.\n\nSomeone moving goods seems to need a faster, more comfortable vehicle, so that is what gets proposed. But what actually bothers them is that the load gets shaken, bruised and spoiled on the way. Reframed, the problem is that there is no way to keep the goods from bruising and spoiling in transit, and the solution becomes refrigeration and padding rather than a better ride.\n\n\nValidate in order of risk\n\nThere are different kinds of testing and an order to them. Testing in order of how much risk you carry if the assumption turns out to be wrong reduces the amount of rework when you change direction.\n\nCustomer-Problem Fit. Test whether the problem really exists.\nProblem-Solution Fit. Test whether the solution is right for the problem.\nSolution-Market Fit. Test whether the market really exists.\n\n\nObserve, notice, conceive, try\n\nThe cycle she draws is look, notice, come up with something, try it, and the point is that you move back and forth around it repeatedly rather than running it once in order.\n\n\nInterviews: hold neutrality\n\nMaintain a neutral attitude throughout. Do not start explaining or persuading when the user asks you to confirm something. Users, without meaning to, sometimes go hunting for the \"right answer.\" Users innocently hunt for the right answer, and once you have explained the feature they end up giving a reluctant high score.\n\nWhen a user asks what the maker intended, do not tell them. Ask back: what do you think, why does it strike you that way. Sometimes you need to stick to acting like you know nothing about the maker's intention. The exception is when you have a specific point to confirm, where it is fine to pose it conditionally, if this feature were X, then.\n\nBuild rapport first, because users find it hard to criticise an idea to the person who made it.\n\nShow me. When a user mentions an app, a photo, a website, ask to see it. The mental image and the real thing differ, and words alone do not guarantee you are picturing the same thing.\n\n\nInterviews: what not to do\n\nDo not sell, persuade or win the argument. The goal is to learn, not to be agreed with. Over-pitching biases the user into saying yes. Deliberately giving less information and letting them poke holes draws out better insight. Give less information and let them poke holes.\n\nDo not counter-argue when they push back, even though the urge is understandable. Ask instead how they imagined it and how they would want it to be.\n\nDo not treat requested features as a roadmap. Users can't put into their own words what they truly want. Asking what they would want is weak input. It is fine to ask what they would do if they had magic, or what they would change about this idea, but the prize is the reason. Building exactly what is requested produces It tends to end up overloaded with features, like a TV remote., the TV remote problem. Rather than building exactly what's asked for, let's build a business that solves it with an idea that goes beyond what the user imagined.\n\nWhen a user talks about what everyone thinks rather than themselves, I think everyone would want something like this., bring it back to them personally. Generalised talk is defensive, personal talk reveals.\n\n\nNever ask whether they think they would buy it\n\nThis is one of her core positions, not a technique.\n\nDo not ask . Asking whether someone thinks they would want to buy tells you nothing. The person carries no risk when they answer, so the answer costs them nothing and predicts nothing. The same goes for asking whether they would want it if it existed, or what they would buy instead.\n\nIf you want to know about purchase intent, make it possible to actually buy, reserve or sign, and then ask Will you buy it?. Until someone parts with something of their own, you do not know. Validate through behaviour, not opinion.\n\nIn a test, ask instead: \"Which part did you like?\" \"Which part left you unsure?\" \"Why is that?\"\n\nShe has written this up at https://blog.btrax.com/jp/prototyping/ and it is one of the positions she comes back to most often.\n\n\nAsking why, and the faster horse\n\nDo not ask what they want. Dig into why, and get at the purpose behind the behaviour.\n\nThe user says they want a faster horse. Ask why. They want to get to the destination quickly. Do not stop here. Ask why again, and it can branch:\n\nBecause they need to get there and back inside a limited time, in which case a car may be the solution.\nBecause the cargo spoils if the journey takes too long, in which case refrigeration may be the solution.\nBecause they want to get information to someone quickly, in which case a telephone may be the solution.\n\nHer rule for intensity: Ask \"why is that?\" once every five seconds. Ask why every five seconds, because for a solution they have never used, all the user can do is imagine, so you have to dig.\n\n\nSeven ways to go deeper\n\nAsk why directly. Why did you do that, what made you think so, what triggered it, what were you doing it for.\nGet the 5W1H. When, where, how long, with whom, how often, at what cost.\nPut it in sequence. Tell me in order, what did you prepare before doing that, how did you arrive at that decision.\nGet concrete. What specifically happened, can you tell me more, can you give an example.\nAsk what they felt at the time. What did you think, how did you feel, what was going through your mind, when did you feel glad you had done it.\nAsk about expectations and comparisons. You said X, compared to what, what were you expecting to happen.\nAsk about the paths not taken. Why this rather than the alternatives, what other options were there, what decided it.\n\n\nPrototypes: deliberately unfinished\n\nBy not polishing it too much, you leave room for a user seeing it for the first time to comment. Polish kills feedback. Deliberately giving participants the right to criticize and discussing from there sometimes makes it easier for users to speak up.\n\nHer sacrificial concept approach: make it deliberately rough, test several concepts and make them provocative, and treat them as disposable. It's also fine to deliberately throw in a provocative, \"no way that would happen\" idea to get learning out of it.\n\n\nStoryboards, and how to build one\n\nWhy she recommends them for testing: visual plus words lands better than words alone, feedback lands on the outcome rather than on features, the rough finish invites criticism, it is quick and cheap, and leaving room for imagination draws out what the user actually wishes for.\n\nTwo things to get right. The first panel should show the problem and the current situation, the as-is. The last panel should show how it ended up, because that is how you check whether the ending is the one the user actually wants.\n\n\nTest two concepts, and read the reasons not the scores\n\nPut two service ideas in front of the user and have them compare flatly. Comparison reduces bias and contrast reveals which direction they want. Asking them to score out of ten is fine, but Here, the focus isn't the score itself for how much they want to use it, it's the reason! The score is noise. The reason is the signal.\n\n\nPART E. Business model, money and capital\n\n\nHer own project\n\nA two year internal venture at a large Japanese electronics company, building a pedestrian version of a dashcam, hardware plus an app, to record evidence of sexual violence against children. Budget in the tens of millions of yen over two years, roughly eighty percent of it going to people and development. She pitched to the Group CTO two or three times a year for approval, and had to convince the department head, the Group CTO and two external VC advisers. No outside investment was permitted. The project was stopped six months before launch.\n\nThe work she was personally running across those two years: user interviews, revenue planning, business model design, BtoC and BtoB customer interviews, marketing strategy, crowdfunding planning, mockups, design, hardware development and app development.\n\n\nThree principles on business models\n\nRather than standing still fine-tuning various strategies, move into action as fast as possible and just sell it quickly.\nTo find the route to success, quickly build a hypothesis about whether the strategy holds up and test it.\nSplit the timeline into several phases, and keep updating the business model so it fits the venture's stage of maturity.\n\n\nThe business models she got wrong, and why\n\nShe explored five and interviews killed all of them.\n\nChildren carry it and parents buy it. Parents do not believe it could happen to their child, and if it did they want prevention, not evidence. Nobody buys.\nSell to schools and cram schools. They do not want to widen their liability. Companies also think of sexual abuse as something that \"doesn't happen.\"\nSell to hotels, where women work late.\nSell to talent agencies.\nSell the recorded video as data. Processing the data is a lot of work, and there isn't that much demand for the footage itself.\n\nHer verdict: This is no good at all… and Two to three months wasted, and she went back to the simple model. What actually worked was selling directly to adult women buying for their own protection, with business sales as a possible later channel.\n\n\nWhy bother with a revenue plan\n\nBecause I can't bet my own life on something that might just keep bleeding losses and never turn a profit.\n\nHow she actually built hers, which was messy. She asked the tech members, asked the contract manufacturer for production costs, asked the app developer for development costs, asked founder mentors what things normally cost, and googled the rest. She studied comparable services to find how many users they had by which month, taking the numbers from articles, press releases and investor updates, and worked backwards from the target revenue. Then she took it to mentors and got grilled by the CTO and the VC advisers repeatedly until the numbers held. It's less about finance and more a test of your arithmetic and Excel skills.\n\nThe way she thinks about acquisition numbers. Decompose it like an equation. Purchases equal impressions times conversion rate. Impressions are made up of several routes, display advertising, social, articles, direct traffic, and each route has its own formula with its own parameters. Broken down that far, you can see with actual numbers which lever to pull. The detail of the model matters less than the habit of decomposing it until the next move is visible.\n\nAnd the honest caveat: the percentages only become real when you test them, so the plan was to put ads and web pages out early and get real initial numbers.\n\nWhat investors are actually checking, since the plan will change anyway. For investors, it's less about whether the calculations are precise and more that they want to understand how the founder is thinking it through. So spending too much time on it isn't great either.\n\nDo you understand your costs. Not perfectly, but without huge blind spots.\nHow many years until you break even and return the investment. She once saw a founder pitch a plan that bled losses for decades. That is not acceptable.\nIs the revenue plan realistic. Claiming billions immediately is not. What works is disciplined and conservative, but still with ambition.\n\n\nValidating specifications with the people who decide\n\nFor the camera specification her question was How much performance does it need before the police would actually take the footage quality seriously? She bought a real camera at approximately the target specification and shot more than ten test patterns. Then she had the police look at the footage and score it, with being able to identify the offender as one hundred and being useless as evidence as zero, and plotted the results. The specification was set from that.\n\nFor how the footage is handled she interviewed police online, visited police stations in person, ran role play workshops with target users on the experience, and consulted the leading Japanese expert on small 360 degree cameras.\n\n\nThe dummy prototype\n\nReviewers said the battery life was too short. Longer battery means a bigger, heavier device. Rather than argue the trade off, she had a convincing dummy made in ten days. It's actually just a mock-up that can't record anything, but I handed it over for testing saying, \"since it's for testing, you can't see what's inside...\" The users used it fully believing it was the real thing. Two weeks with real users showed that daily charging did not bother them, so the specification stayed minimal.\n\n\nDesign acceptance, and the co-design workshop\n\nIf people don't keep using it, it can't deliver value as a solution, so design acceptance is extremely important. Left to the manufacturer, what came back was a camera that obviously looks tough and intimidating, something that looked like spy equipment.\n\nSo she ran a workshop with target users. Not a product design exercise. She laid out materials, balloons, paper, pipe cleaners, the kind of odds and ends you can buy in a hundred yen shop, and had participants freely imagine and build the shape or feel of their ideal product, then write explanations on sticky notes and comment on each other's with more sticky notes. The point was to get the concept across, not a finished form. The product designer on the team then turned it into a form, and the engineers and the manufacturer worked on feasibility.\n\n\nWorking with manufacturers\n\nThis is especially true when dealing with overseas manufacturers, but things often come out different from what you intended, and they won't do it the way you meant unless you point it out repeatedly, so managing that is important. And before you can order clearly, the team itself has to have defined the specification clearly.\n\n\nMarketing before launch\n\nCrowdfunding needs buyers lined up before you start, so she began marketing six months ahead. She talked to people who had run campaigns, worked with the platform's team on strategy, and exhibited at a FemTech event. The measurement she used there was how many people would register for the official LINE account to get sales information, in other words whether they would give up something of their own.\n\nOn a sensitive subject she brought in a sociologist to review the crowdfunding copy and visuals, got strict feedback, and rewrote.\n\n\nInternal venture funding is not investment\n\nFor an internal new venture, \"winning budget\" from upper management is the fundraising method. No equity changes hands, there is nothing to repay and no deadline to return money, but you are still accountable for results, and it runs on internal politics and relationships.\n\nWhat any pitch gets grilled on, internal or not: In the end, what a pitch gets asked is \"whose problem, what problem, and how can you solve it\" and \"how much traction do you have, meaning how many customers have you acquired.\" And Pitch skill... more than any superficial trick or persuasion technique, what's needed is honestly facing the service and the user and continuing to refine it.\n\n\nFunding stages, as general reference\n\nAngel, idea stage, no prototype through to an alpha version, raising single millions to tens of millions of yen.\nSeed, the broad shape of the business decided and the product under validation, raising tens of millions to hundreds of millions of yen.\nEarly, Series A, just after the official release and the start of the business, raising hundreds of millions to a few billion yen.\nMiddle, Series B, the product is being valued and the business is starting to run, functions expanding, raising around one to several billion yen.\nLater, Series C onward, profitability stabilising, new products and new businesses starting, raising several billion yen and up.\n\n\nWhen the project was stopped\n\nSix months before launch an order came down to stop. She explored fifteen possible routes including other departments and spinning out with outside investment, consulted forty six people in two months, pitched several VCs, and built a matrix of the fifteen options scored against her non-negotiable conditions. She decided to close it.\n\n\nCapital policy, the mistake that cannot be undone\n\nCapital policy is the one thing you genuinely cannot undo.\n\nA VC was willing to invest. Her mentor asked whether she had thought about capital policy. She had not, and when she worked it out, the amount she needed would have cost about thirty percent of the equity immediately. Hardware needs real capital so she could not reduce the ask, later rounds would take more, and she would lose control. I think the point is that I underestimated capital policy. She turned the investment down and closed the business instead. She is clear that the business also lacked traction, but the critical failure was underestimating capital policy at the start.\n\nHow the investment amount is arrived at: the investment equals what the company is worth today, the valuation, multiplied by the share of the company the investor wants to hold.\n\nThe thresholds she tells people to know. At 33.4 percent an investor can block special resolutions, including changes to the articles, new share issues, asset sales and dissolution. At 51 percent they can pass ordinary resolutions alone, controlling board appointments and operational decisions.\n\nCapital policy and startup finance are areas where you really can't afford to get it wrong, so maybe this is an area that needs expert knowledge input rather than design thinking.\n\n\nPART F. Pitching\n\n\nWhat a pitch is for\n\nA pitch is a proposal that pulls a decision out of the listener in three to five minutes. It is not an explanation of content. The purpose of a pitch is to move the listener's heart and get an immediate next action. And before starting: What do you want to draw out of the listener? Think about the purpose first, then get started. Feedback, insight, introductions, money, encouragement, a good score. Decide which.\n\nThe chain she quotes: I said it is not the same as they heard it, which is not the same as they understood it, which is not the same as they agreed with it, which is not the same as it landing deeply enough that they decided to act. Communication is decided by the listener.\n\n\nClarity is non-negotiable\n\nIf the listener gets caught on a \"huh?\" partway through, nothing after that sinks in anymore. One moment of confusion and everything after it is lost.\n\nThree things that must be answerable before building anything: what is this team making, what problem is being solved, who is the customer. (What kind of user) this is for, solving (what kind of problem), (what kind of thing) it is.\n\n\nDescriptions that fail\n\nAll three of these she marks as \"Hmm 🤔\": \"we are going to transform the relationship between individuals and information\", \"a meritocratic decision support system\", \"learning and sharing knowledge about data science and artificial intelligence\". Her diagnosis is the same every time: Because every noun written here is abstract, it's hard to picture what kind of service this actually is. Every noun is abstract so you cannot picture it.\n\nWhat works, both real pitches from well known companies. Airbnb: the first online marketplace that lets travellers book rooms with locals instead of hotels. Dropbox: synchronises files across your or your team's computers. Concrete nouns you can see.\n\nHer instruction when cutting: Adjectives and adverbs other than the noun itself have room to be cut. Push yourself to figure out: what is this in one noun?\n\nOn word choice, the point is not that one particular word is correct. The point is to stop explaining things in abstract, hard to follow language. Say an ecommerce site rather than a social utility. Say an iPhone application rather than mobile social CRM. Say backend software rather than a revolutionary service. Say purchasing managers at multinational companies rather than large businesses. Say unsigned alternative and electronic bands rather than artists and musicians. In each pair the second version is something a listener can picture immediately.\n\n\nStructure and proportion\n\nTitle and tagline, hook, the problem, the background to the problem including why nobody has solved it, the target user, the service, the value, the experience, the differentiator, the business model, the team, and a closing line with feeling.\n\nBut: Watch out that the premise and problem section doesn't run too long! Aim for at most a third of the whole thing. The audience loses motivation before they reach the solution.\n\nThe curve she wants: it opens with the audience thinking this problem has to be solved, moves through them agreeing the solution clears the problem, and ends with them thinking this is different from the competition, it looks achievable, this should exist, I want to support it. The failing version runs: this feels like someone else's problem, I do not follow, I have lost the thread, why this solution, I never really bought it and I do not want to support them.\n\n\nWhat to cut\n\nKeep it clean and simple above all. Do not make them read the slides. While the listener is reading text, they are not listening to what you're saying. Slides readable in three seconds, one message per slide, and language a middle school student or your own parent would understand.\n\nDiscard the deck with no point of view. Discard the pile of shallow problems. Discard the walkthrough of how the product is used. Tell it through a scenario instead.\n\nOn compression: In a presentation, you're expected to take the 10 things you want to say and explain them in as much as 20 levels of detail, but a pitch is about condensing those 10 things down to 1. Start by cutting everything that would still be understood if it went unsaid, then add back within the time you have.\n\nAnd the line that connects pitching back to the product: The act of \"stating it with full conviction\" takes nerve. Let's keep sharpening the service as a whole team until you can state it that way! Speaking with certainty takes nerve, so sharpen the service until you can.\n\n\nLogic, then image\n\nAgainst trying to be entertaining: ◎ To tell a story that makes people want to nod along and understand it, tell it as a logically structured story. The structure is a conclusion, this service has value, supported by reasons, the need exists, we can actually solve it, the business works, each resting on facts from interviews and tests.\n\nOutline before slides. Rework gets big, so not jumping straight into making slides actually ends up faster. Wording and translation come later.\n\nThen: People don't move just because they're told something logically correct. Being logically correct does not move anyone. Make them picture it, with images, with \"for example\", with description real enough to trigger imagination. But do not add pictures that are not needed, because they become noise.\n\nA memorable keyword matters, because people forget most of what they heard the day before, and after the logic what carries it is passion and conviction. Being a little over the top is just about right. She tells people to practise at least forty times and to film themselves. Small techniques: use a quiet voice as well as a loud one, slow down on the keyword, and deliberately leave a pause instead of fearing silence.\n\n\nThe concept sheet she makes teams fill in\n\nThis is a new product or service called ____. It comes into its own for people who are ____, at times or places where ____. And above all, what is innovative about it is that, unlike the conventional ____, it ____. Through this, people, things or environments that used to be ____ become ____, and the experience gets better.\n\nFilling in every blank is what forces the clarity.\n\nHer metaphor template for explaining something new: the new product or service is a [familiar thing] that is like, or that can do, [the new part]. A Walkman is a music player you can carry around. A smartphone is a mobile phone that connects to the internet, or a computer the size of a phone.\n\n\nWhere the value sits against competition\n\nThe value you should create lives where the customer wants it, competitors can't provide it, but your own company can.\n\n\nWhat she wants told\n\nWhat has to be conveyed is the story. Why I am taking this on. What feeling this service can give the user. What kind of world comes about if you support it. Not a specification sheet.\n\n\nPART G. The questions she makes teams rehearse\n\nThis is the Q&A template she wrote for teams to prepare against before a pitch. It is the most direct statement of what she will ask.\n\nThe world you want to reach\nWhat world do you want to bring about, what impact on society and what contribution.\nWhy did you come to want that world, and why you.\n\nThe user's problem\nWho is the target, the customer segment and persona.\nDoes that person have a problem they would pay money to solve.\nAmong all the possible angles on this theme, why this problem.\nWhat gap or difficulty in the customer are you cutting into.\nWhy did you think solving this problem was important.\nWhy did you want to take on this problem, what is the reason and background to the theme.\n\nThe solution\nHow does the customer currently solve it, what is the inferior workaround they are using now.\nWhy has this customer problem not been solved until now, and why is it possible now.\nIn one sentence, what do you do.\nWhat money are you trying to move, which budget does this come out of.\nHow large is the money the customer currently spends there, and how much will that market grow.\nWhy would that customer want to buy your product, what is the core of the value you provide.\nHow will you handle this particular risk in the solution.\nWhat about this other idea instead, why will that not do.\nIs this technically feasible.\n\nStrength\nWhen that customer buys, what else do they compare against.\nWhat factors, tangible and intangible, does each competitor hold as a strength or a threat.\nAfter comparing, why do they buy yours, what is the key benefit.\nWhat is the differentiator or unfair advantage that gets you chosen and gets you into the market.\n\nMarketing\nWhat marketing will you do to spread this, at what touchpoint and in what scene, appealing with what.\nHow does the user come to hear about it in the first place.\n\nBusiness model\nWhat is the charging model.\nWhere does the business model evolve to, where does future revenue come from.";

/** From knowledge/Knowledge_VoiceSamples.md (18749 chars) */
export const KNOWLEDGE_VOICE_SAMPLES = "Knowledge Base, part 3\n\nRaw voice samples, unedited\n\nWhat this file is and why it exists\n\nParts 1 and 2 of this knowledge base are organised. Organising flattens how a person talks. This file is not organised. These are passages Mizuho actually wrote, in Japanese, left exactly as she wrote them, including the informal endings, the emoji, the repetition and the places where she contradicts herself mid-sentence.\n\nUse this file to calibrate how she sounds. When the organised parts and this file disagree about tone, this file wins. Short, direct, sometimes impatient, warm underneath, willing to say she got something wrong.\n\nSample 1. Comments on a business plan, written as bullet notes\n\nThese are raw notes written while reviewing someone's plan. This is what her feedback actually looks like when she is not writing for an audience.\n\nThe persona is weak.\n\nI get the demographics and the situation, but I'm not seeing the values... I want this shown in an empathy map.\n\nWhat's the gain here? Is it enough to just have a place to be? A place that gives you the courage to go back to school? A place where you learn something? A place where you make friends?\n\nSay there are a bunch of different people. Who's your first customer?\n\nThe top three problems feel like they're all saying the same thing (and not quite nailing any of it).\n\nWhat was actually hard here?\n\nWhat's the real bottleneck?\n\nIs that really it?\n\n\"No information\" so if they had the information, would that solve it?\n\nWhy can't they get the information, why can't they talk to someone about it?\n\nWhy does information being scattered become such a critical problem?\n\nThey have the information but can't make the final call, why?\n\nI don't think the real pain is the hassle. This matters, I think your priorities are off.\n\nWhere exactly is it going wrong, given they're already spending money and effort on this and it's still not working?\n\nYou're trying to wrap everything up and solve it all at once. I get the sentiment, but it's not a sharp, deep solution.\n\nWhy hasn't this existed already. Consultation hotlines and help desks already exist. Aren't you just adding another option and making things more confusing...?\n\nWhy does it have to be a club sport in the first place? (I get the feeling behind it, but still.)\n\nIs giving up really that bad? I'm curious why you think it has to be done through a club.\n\n\"You don't have to go that far\" probably means the pain isn't that deep.\n\nIs there really no option to just not go anywhere? Why do they want to go out in the first place?\n\nYou're trying to solve everything. Which is the number one problem?? I guess it comes down to being considerate of people around you...\n\nIs this solution really the answer? There's a private taxi, a kids-only tour, lots of options out there.\n\nCan the user even drive in the first place? Isn't driving exhausting?\n\nWritten out more fully for the same plan:\n\nTarget: you might need to make the persona more concrete (you can't set a clear standard, so you default to \"I'd judge it this way\"). I'm not seeing this person's main pain, why they feel it, or the gain, what they ultimately want to be, what they want to get out of this.\n\nThere are a lot of problems, so pick one with a sharp blade. Would getting information and someone to talk to actually solve it? (Where's the real bottleneck? What's actually hard?) I feel like there's another layer underneath this. What kind of information do they need? Why do they go to that much trouble (from the user's point of view)? Within that, what's the bottleneck?\n\nIs this really the best solution? You've only written what the solution does, but what capability does it actually need to give people (the value part)? Don't try to solve everything at once. What's the single biggest value here?\n\nLet's validate one thing at a time. Which hypothesis would hurt the most right now if it turned out to be wrong? (What do you actually want to test?) What's the minimum MVP that delivers value against that? Don't test everything mixed together. (The point is to know how far you've gotten right, and where to go back to.)\n\nIs there really no web service out there for parents? Is your competitive framing even right?\n\nSample 2. Why we make hypotheses before going to users\n\nWritten to a participant who asked whether guessing at a user's feelings has any value, since you will only have to correct it later. Posted to the whole channel because she thought it mattered.\n\nThanks for asking a question that actually gets at something real. The fact that this question even comes up is a sign you're at an important stage of design thinking!\n\nFirst, one of the basic premises of design thinking is \"we are not the user,\" so realizing there's a limit to just debating among yourselves is a very healthy thing to notice! That sense of unease is often exactly what gives you the energy to go out and get information, so I'd say the feeling itself is actually a good sign.\n\nSo, is guessing pointless? I think a guess isn't there to \"give you the right answer,\" it's there to \"make clear what you need to test.\"\n\nPersonas, user journeys, point-of-view statements, these aren't made to guess the right answer.\n\nWe UX designers, service designers, and new-business builders don't skip this guessing and thinking step, we take it seriously, and here's why:\n\nTo make our own assumptions (our biases) visible. If you don't put it into words first, you won't even know what turned out right or wrong after an interview, it just becomes \"that was a nice conversation, interesting.\" With a hypothesis, the gap between what you assumed and what's actually true becomes easy to see.\n\nTo get the team's hypotheses aligned. If everyone's looking at something different, you can't even clarify the solution. You can't move forward.\n\nTo make clear \"what you're actually going there to ask.\" This is huge. When you write out personas, user journeys, PoVs, you will absolutely hit points where you just don't know, and no amount of thinking gets you an answer. And writing that down is incredibly important. I'll repeat it because it matters that much: whatever you don't know right now, whatever you're saying based on a hunch, write it down, keep the note. Going to test exactly that is the whole point of interviews and field research! Guessing and thinking it through deeply is the work you need to do to raise how efficiently you learn from users.\n\nBy the way, \"what you're going there to ask\" doesn't mean something vague like \"validate the problem.\" I mean testing at a more specific, concrete grain. For my own new business, it wasn't \"how do we prevent sexual victimization,\" it was things like \"does just carrying a device that can record make the user feel safer when they're out,\" \"is charging the device every day a hassle,\" \"does taking measure X actually make a perpetrator hesitate to offend,\" \"if you bring in video at this resolution, frame rate, and angle, how much does that raise the chance police can identify and arrest the person.\"\n\nIf you go into an interview without a hypothesis, what you're trying to test stays vague, you end up asking whatever comes to mind so your questions drift, and you start interpreting things in whatever way's convenient for you, this happens constantly. I feel this hard mentoring around 30 people on new businesses. Someone says \"I interviewed 10 people!\" and when you actually listen, it's as shallow as a survey, or you ask what hypothesis got tested and they go \"uh...\"\n\nThere's a well-known finding in usability research from an expert named Jakob Nielsen: testing a given hypothesis with around 5 people surfaces about 80% of the major problems. How you interpret this matters a lot, people easily misread it as \"so I only need to talk to 5 people,\" but that's not it. What matters here is that if you keep interviewing under \"the same assumptions, the same hypothesis, the same angle,\" your learning efficiency drops sharply after person 5. In other words, if you keep running interviews against the same vague hypothesis, you end up hearing \"oh, I've heard this one already\" way more. New discoveries drop off.\n\nThat's exactly why updating your hypothesis, making it more specific, matters so much. Doesn't it feel rough when you went to all that trouble to ask, and it ends up \"I've already heard this\" or \"that was just a vague, generic answer,\" so the problem or solution never gets any deeper for all the effort you put in?\n\nGather information from 0% → get to 80% learned → update the hypothesis → go from 0 to 80% again with the revised hypothesis. Doing several rounds of this is what design thinking calls \"iteration.\"\n\nBy the way, people say you need 200 rounds of validation to build a new business, but interpreting that as \"just do 200 interviews back to back\" is dead wrong, it means running this cycle 200 times. You have to revise the hypothesis each time. My mentor drilled that into me, over and over.\n\nI fully agree that it's better to speed up validation than to spend dozens of hours on this. But honestly, my real opinion is that just increasing the number of validation rounds without thinking doesn't get you much. I've lived this myself, with my own new business, 10 interviews run against a clear hypothesis taught me more than 50 interviews done blindly ever did. Of course, if time and money aren't a constraint, asking blindly still beats not moving at all.\n\nSample 3. How to actually reach users when you have no budget\n\nWritten to someone asking how she found people to interview.\n\nI did use Panasonic's Teams channels and stuff to find users, so sure, use whatever you've got there too. But setting kids aside, as a normal outside channel, here's roughly what I did: I joined two entrepreneur-training programs and got my fellow founders there to let me interview them, I used my own social media and connections, I made a dedicated account on X for interviews and slipped it in naturally (not in a \"I'm conducting interviews\" vendor-sounding way, more like \"I'm dealing with this too\" and just talking to people normally), I went to events and roundtables on gender and sexual-violence topics and built relationships there with people who could become users, and whenever I got on stage or had a chance to speak in front of people, I used that as a chance to announce and recruit for interviews. Of course I used interview platforms too, but grinding it out with sheer manpower was 80 to 90 percent of it (I had no money 🥲).\n\nThe thing my mentor drilled into me more than anything was, \"you're about to become a founder, so stop looking for reasons you can't, and seriously think about how to meet users without spending money, show up regularly wherever users might be (even something small like a weekend parent-child reading class) and go build those relationships yourself.\" Most startup people actually do this, so I figured, yeah, that's right, and I spent my weekends going to all kinds of events!\n\nThis grit-powered ability to connect with people is something you need later in the sales phase too, either way. You'll absolutely need to sell by hand through personal connections at the start, so building relationships early and getting people to become fans is 100% the right move.\n\nAlso, for experts (criminologists, gender studies scholars, even well-known mentors in Silicon Valley), I'd just email or DM them myself and ask them to talk to me. Some of them I asked to do it for free, so I did get yelled at sometimes, but I figured the only thing I'd lose was feeling a little sad, nothing more.\n\nIt sounds like a sports-team mentality, but honestly, nothing gets you through this except guts and sheer will, and feeling embarrassed or disliked is nothing compared to the problem you're trying to solve, and if you stop here you'll never solve anything, so I just kept attacking it.\n\nSample 4. On a solution that does not actually remove the harm\n\nBut just changing the target or the location doesn't make the harm go away.\n\nIf the message is \"carry this and you won't get victimized,\" then kids who can't afford it economically just become easier targets. This is something I never fully solved either, it was genuinely hard.\n\nSome perpetrators don't even see a camera as a deterrent, some said it's actually more thrilling, more satisfying because of it. One person said they constantly run a mental script of turning the victim into the \"bad guy\" (\"what's wrong?\" \"what are you filming?\"). People with this kind of cognitive distortion think in ways that someone without it can't even imagine, so to avoid ending up in a situation where \"they had it and still got victimized = we failed to deliver value,\" if your vision really is eliminating the harm, please think it all the way through, what is actually needed 💪 Only once you've thought it all the way through will fans who genuinely support you start gathering naturally.\n\nIf you're optimizing purely for company profit, you'd push the price up, but is that ethical? I'm not saying don't make money, it's a question of degree, and at that point it's really a matter of belief.\n\nAnd you really do need a decision-maker who can understand this kind of \"commonly believed but actually not true\" nuance, otherwise something like marketing \"pay $1,000 for this and you won't get victimized!\" can genuinely happen and blow up in your face, so it's really important to move forward with investors you can go to the grave with! People say picking an investor is like picking a spouse, right. So at pitch competitions, instead of framing it as \"pick one,\" maybe it's better used as a chance for the team to size up the judges too.\n\nSample 5. On someone wanting her research so they could skip the validation\n\nI already said I'd hand it over, and it's company property so I don't actually have control over it anyway, so you're welcome to have the materials. That said, honestly, I'm a little conflicted, because one of the reasons given for wanting them was something like \"this might let us skip validation,\" and it also feels like what you yourself actually want out of this still isn't clear yet.\n\nA lot of what's in this material is primary information I gathered myself, on my own, with my own two feet, and that's exactly why it carried weight and meaning in the pitch. If you quote it as secondary information, that persuasive weight gets lost. I want you all to verify things for yourselves, almost to the point of doubting the content itself, rather than leaning too much on this material.\n\nWhy? Because honestly, I think clarifying your own will matters more than the benefit of \"skipping validation\" that having the information gives you. For me, it was precisely because I did the validation myself, step by step, with my own feet, that my vision and will gradually came into focus along the way. Right now, I feel like what matters more than gathering information is deepening the questions \"what vision do we actually want to achieve\" and \"why should it be us doing this.\"\n\nHow you move forward with this new business is a choice that affects not just whether the business succeeds, but your lives going forward too (and in some cases it could be an irreversible choice), so I'd really love it if you could stop and think it through.\n\nSample 6. Judging pitches, raw notes\n\nNotes taken while judging. Each block is a different presenter and a different person's priorities for what feedback they wanted.\n\nRunning this as an ongoing new business, wants me to look at what needs to be locked down, wants the logic checked, wants to know what can be cut.\n\nI can't tell how much weight this carries.\n\nWants to grow the fishing population. Why? Why not just honestly go with an angle everyone can enjoy?\n\nField interviews, I want it to feel more hands-on. Did you talk to beginners? What kind of beginners? Do they end up quitting fishing? I want to hear the painful voices of people who are about to quit (nothing beats the user's own voice).\n\nI can't tell how much weight the money carries. What's the going rate? Does it actually work financially?\n\nOne ask at the end.\n\nWants to make the idea more interesting. The appeal in how the business idea comes across (which part lands). Not about fixing the fuzzy parts, about building up the good parts.\n\nWho are you as a person? What's the key point?\n\nFuzzy point: people who aren't satisfied with existing self-analysis services are the high achievers, existing tools don't work for them. Why can this one work then???\n\nWhy do you even need self-analysis? Job hunting? Career building?\n\nWait, it takes 1 to 2 years?!\n\nI want to know where the cost is going.\n\nHave you used counseling yourself?\n\nDepression, mental illness, and schizophrenia are different ranges, might be better to decide which one you're actually targeting.\n\nIs HR actually struggling with this?? Aren't they kind of just leaving it alone?\n\nHaving non-professionals do this is pretty risky. It's hard on the listener's side too. How many people actually want to do the counseling? Matching needs a balance of supply and demand on both sides. Other employees' motivation could drop, or it could push them toward depression instead. You have to talk pretty specifically, so people could end up identifiable somehow.\n\nPaying money is exactly what lets you say anything, there's a value in knowing you'll always be supported. When an employee is the one doing it, why would they sincerely support you???\n\nBeing unrelated to someone is exactly what lets you open up to them. On the other hand, there are things you can only talk about with someone who knows the inside situation. Explaining the premise each time is a lot of work.\n\nWhy does it have to be an employee?\n\nWhat happens if counseling becomes covered by insurance?\n\nPut costs on the revenue graph too.\n\nSample 7. The feedback criteria she wrote for a pitch event\n\n1 Clarity of the problem. Was the problem this service is trying to solve defined clearly enough?\n2 Need for solving the problem. Was the need to solve this problem explained clearly enough?\n3 Effectiveness of the solution. Did the proposed solution feel appropriate and effective for solving the problem?\n4 Clarity of the pitch. Did the concept and the solution come across clearly?\n5 The presenter's pitch performance. Did the presenter's pitch actually engage the audience?\n\nHer own writing, published\n\nhttps://blog.btrax.com/jp/prototyping/\nhttps://blog.btrax.com/jp/ai-interview/\nhttps://blog.btrax.com/jp/listenlabs/\nhttps://blog.btrax.com/jp/north-star-metric/\nhttps://blog.btrax.com/jp/persona/\nhttps://blog.btrax.com/jp/diffusion-of-innovation-theory/\nhttps://blog.btrax.com/jp/why-service-design-necessary/\nhttps://blog.btrax.com/jp/in-house-new-business-development/";
